docs/tables-policy.md:5:The solo UI has no result table. `GameStatus.tsx` displays four properties of a single game in a definition list. Tournament mode adds bracket and past-event result tables; both use captions and column headers under the policy below. Reproduction controls use forms, and the board is a CSS grid of buttons.
docs/competition-research.md:9:The working setup was deliberately modest: one browser game, people seated close enough to see the screen and paper notes beside the device. At the time of this playtest, event sketches were discussed on paper because the game had no participant entry or bracket controller. Tournament mode now supplies those features alongside the ordinary card play and reproduction tools.
docs/dialog-policy.md:5:Solo New Game, Restart, board-code loading and snapshot loading call the controller directly. Tournament's Leave tournament action uses a native dialog before discarding an unfinished event.
docs/dialog-policy.md:9:A confirmation is useful only when it corresponds to a real decision about abandoning work. Its content and focus need to make the conservative path understandable before the player commits to leaving. Leaving unfinished work asks first in a native dialog element with two buttons, Keep playing and Leave tournament. Keep playing takes focus when it opens, and Escape keeps playing. The decision is about the unfinished work being left; opening a disclosure to inspect a code is not itself that decision.
docs/dialog-policy.md:11:The confirmation has two actions: Keep playing and Leave tournament. In particular, the snapshot and Tournament exporters do not turn this confirmation into an automatic save workflow. Export is a separate user action with a text result. The confirmation does not imply that an unsaved draft has been preserved.
docs/dialog-policy.md:13:Tournament owns the current leave route. Its confirmation explains that unfinished progress will be discarded, focuses Keep playing when opened, and treats Escape as Keep playing. Focus policy covers orientation when the Tournament screen replaces the solo controls.
docs/dialog-policy.md:37:A mismatch stores its remaining active milliseconds in game selection. The solo session clock advances it when the page is visible and the imported-pause flag is clear. Tournament uses its separate clock adapter and pauses it while the leave dialog is open.
docs/dialog-policy.md:39:The solo session has no confirmation surface and keeps its existing timing behavior. Tournament pauses its active turn clock while the Leave tournament confirmation is open, including a pending mismatch interval, and resumes with a fresh clock observation when the person keeps playing. Clock design and Mismatch interval explain the established clock model.
docs/dialog-policy.md:49:After a decision, the durability of the source control matters. Existing action buttons generally remain mounted after a deal replacement, while Resume removes itself. Game handles Resume by moving focus before the button is removed. Tournament returns focus to its Leave tournament button when the dialog closes, unless confirming departure replaces the whole screen.
docs/dialog-policy.md:61:Current browser tests establish focus retention on ordinary buttons, invalid draft preservation, Resume's transfer order and stale-timer protection. They do not establish this Tournament dialog journey. Any report of dialog coverage has to point at the corresponding implementation and evidence, rather than citing those tests because they also involve focus.
docs/acceptance.md:4:Status: solo game spec version 2.2, with Tournament mode acceptance below.
docs/acceptance.md:37:## Tournament
docs/acceptance.md:41:Export and load a setup draft, an in-progress turn and a finished tournament JSON value. Reject malformed values and unknown fields without replacing the current tournament or draft. Finish an event, reload, open Tournament and inspect Past tournaments. Check the newest-ten retention and the storage-unavailable message.
docs/acceptance.md:43:While an event is active, choose Leave tournament and check that Keep playing is focused. Escape and Keep playing both return to the same turn. Confirm Leave tournament and check that solo play returns with its previous state.
docs/README.md:4:Status: solo game spec version 2.2, with Tournament mode documented as an extension.
docs/README.md:10:Choose a size, then reveal a card and find its partner. Matches stay face up. A miss closes both cards after 800 active milliseconds. Finding every pair completes the game with your moves and time. New Game gives you another deal. Restart repeats the current deal. Tournament mode lets four or eight players take turns on the same deals in a knockout bracket. Full details are in Rules and Tournament mode.
docs/README.md:67:- control-bar.md: The deal and Tournament buttons, size selector, responsive arrangement and session callbacks in Controls.tsx.
docs/README.md:81:- form-layout-guide.md: Label associations, responsive field rows, Tournament player entry, text editing surfaces and load-notice layout.
docs/README.md:124:- persistence-overview.md: What solo preferences and tournament history save, what remains in memory and which persistence page to consult.
docs/README.md:144:- tournament-mode.md: Entry, pairing, turn flow, result comparison, history, storage and tournament JSON.
docs/source-layout.md:17:`Game.tsx` calls `useGame`, passes session values and controller actions into the solo UI, and switches to the Tournament screen while keeping the solo session mounted and its clock inactive. Its output is a single main element containing the heading and either the solo controls, statistics, board and reproduction tools or tournament setup, bracket, JSON tools and history.
docs/source-layout.md:54:`session/browser-clock.ts` defines the 40ms scheduling interval and arithmetic over observed timestamps. `session/use-session-clock.ts` supplies observations, maintains visibility, schedules timeouts and removes pending work on effect cleanup. `session/browser-clock.test.ts` checks the arithmetic independently. `session/use-tournament-clock.ts` applies that active-time arithmetic to tournament turns while the Tournament screen is open.
docs/source-layout.md:56:## Tournament feature
docs/source-layout.md:58:`tournament/rules.ts` creates matches, advances players and compares completed results without browser effects. `bracket.ts` defines four- and eight-player pairings. `model.ts` owns participant, match and result shapes. `validation.ts`, `validation-matches.ts` and `validation-progress.ts` validate tournament exchange and saved records; `format.ts` owns tournament JSON, and `storage.ts` owns the `np3:tournaments` envelope and retention. `use-tournament.ts` connects those rules to React state and browser storage. `Tournament.tsx`, `TournamentSetup.tsx`, `TournamentPlay.tsx`, `TournamentBracket.tsx`, `TournamentTools.tsx` and `TournamentHistory.tsx` render the flow; `tournament.css` owns its layout.
docs/source-layout.md:78:`controls/Controls.tsx` renders the grid-size select, New Game, Tournament and Restart. It reads the size options from `GRID_SIZES`. `controls/control-labels.ts` owns the exact deal, Tournament and reproduction control strings consumed by components and tests.
docs/competition-ties.md:5:A result comparison uses information from completed play. The game core can tell whether a board is complete and can expose its moves and elapsed time. Tournament comparison lives outside the single-game transitions in `src/game/tournament/rules.ts`.
docs/competition-ties.md:31:The seed settles the equality case without an extra round of play, shared outcome or random choice. The single-game core has no opponent or entrant data; the Tournament rules supply seed comparison separately.
docs/competition-ties.md:69:`src/game/move-cap.test.ts` covers completion at the move boundary. The browser and core clock tests cover elapsed behavior separately. Those are single-game rules; the tournament comparator applies their completed move and active-time values in the order defined above.
docs/persistence-overview.md:5:Solo play uses `neon-pairs.preferences.v1` for the selected board size and per-size best moves. Tournament mode uses a separate `np3:tournaments` collection for finished events. Neither key stores an active game. Reloading solo play creates a fresh deal; finished tournament records remain available in Past tournaments.
docs/persistence-overview.md:19:See Preferences store for the exact validation and React effect. Tournament mode and Persistence envelope describe the tournament collection. Best score records covers the gameplay meaning of the solo numbers rather than the browser mechanism that holds them.
docs/persistence-overview.md:23:The solo session includes the game, pause state, session generation, feedback, load notice, announcement counter, celebration flag and preferences. Only the preference portion reaches `writePreferences`. Tournament state is separate; completed records reach `np3:tournaments`, while active tournament progress remains in memory and is not saved by Export. Export places JSON in the field for manual transfer. Neither active game is automatically restored after reload.
import { useEffect, useRef, useState } from 'react';
import { isWon } from '../game.ts';
import { SelectionKind } from '../selection.ts';
import { advanceTournamentGame } from '../tournament/rules.ts';
import type { TournamentState } from '../tournament/model.ts';
import { CLOCK_INTERVAL_MS, createClock, elapsedStep } from './browser-clock.ts';
import type { RefObject } from 'react';

export function useTournamentClock(
  state: TournamentState | null,
  current: RefObject<TournamentState | null>,
  commit: (next: TournamentState) => void,
) {
  const clock = useRef(createClock(performance.now()));
  const visible = useRef(!document.hidden);
  const suspended = useRef(false);
  const [isSuspended, setIsSuspended] = useState(false);
  const started = state?.activeGame?.started ?? false;
  const won = state?.activeGame ? isWon(state.activeGame) : false;

  function running(): boolean {
    const latest = current.current;
    return visible.current && !suspended.current && !!latest?.turnRunning && !!latest.activeGame?.started &&
      !isWon(latest.activeGame);
  }

  function synchronize(): void {
    const step = elapsedStep(clock.current, performance.now(), running());
    clock.current = step.clock;
    const latest = current.current;
    if (step.milliseconds && latest) commit(advanceTournamentGame(latest, step.milliseconds));
  }

  useEffect(() => {
    let active = true;
    let timer: number | undefined;
    function schedule(): void {
      window.clearTimeout(timer);
      const latest = current.current;
      if (!active || !latest?.turnRunning || !latest.activeGame?.started || !running()) return;
      const selection = latest.activeGame.selection;
      const delay = selection.kind === SelectionKind.mismatch
        ? Math.min(CLOCK_INTERVAL_MS, selection.remainingMs)
        : CLOCK_INTERVAL_MS;
      timer = window.setTimeout(() => {
        if (!active) return;
        synchronize();
        schedule();
      }, delay);
    }
    function visibilityChanged(): void {
      if (!active) return;
      synchronize();
      visible.current = !document.hidden;
      schedule();
    }
    schedule();
    document.addEventListener('visibilitychange', visibilityChanged);
    return () => {
      active = false;
      window.clearTimeout(timer);
      document.removeEventListener('visibilitychange', visibilityChanged);
    };
  }, [state?.currentMatch, state?.currentTurn, state?.turnRunning, started, won, isSuspended]);

  return {
    synchronize,
    reset: () => {
      clock.current = createClock(performance.now());
    },

