docs/dialog-policy.md:13:Tournament owns the current leave route. Its confirmation explains that unfinished progress will be discarded, focuses Keep playing when opened, and treats Escape as Keep playing. Focus policy covers orientation when the Tournament screen replaces the solo controls.
docs/dialog-policy.md:37:A mismatch stores its remaining active milliseconds in game selection. The solo session clock advances it when the page is visible and the imported-pause flag is clear. Tournament uses its separate clock adapter and pauses it while the leave dialog is open.
docs/dialog-policy.md:39:The solo session has no confirmation surface and keeps its existing timing behavior. Tournament pauses its active turn clock while the Leave tournament confirmation is open, including a pending mismatch interval, and resumes with a fresh clock observation when the person keeps playing. Clock design and Mismatch interval explain the established clock model.
docs/dialog-policy.md:49:After a decision, the durability of the source control matters. Existing action buttons generally remain mounted after a deal replacement, while Resume removes itself. Game handles Resume by moving focus before the button is removed. Tournament returns focus to its Leave tournament button when the dialog closes, unless confirming departure replaces the whole screen.
docs/dialog-policy.md:61:Current browser tests establish focus retention on ordinary buttons, invalid draft preservation, Resume's transfer order and stale-timer protection. They do not establish this Tournament dialog journey. Any report of dialog coverage has to point at the corresponding implementation and evidence, rather than citing those tests because they also involve focus.
